## Understanding OLED Displays — Why Black Isn't Just a Color

The difference between OLED and LCD screens isn't just marketing. It's a fundamental architectural difference that changes how black and dark colors behave on your phone — and it has real consequences for battery life.

### How LCD Screens Work

LCD (Liquid Crystal Display) panels use a backlight — a uniform light source behind the entire screen. The liquid crystals act as a shutter, blocking varying amounts of that backlight for each pixel to create colors and brightness levels. When an LCD shows a "black" pixel, it's actually blocking the backlight, but the backlight is still on. **An LCD in a dark app uses roughly the same power as an LCD in a bright app.**

### How OLED Screens Work

OLED (Organic Light-Emitting Diode) screens work differently. Each pixel **emits its own light**. There is no backlight. When a pixel needs to be black, it is simply turned **off**. No light, no power consumption.

This means: **on an OLED screen, a pure black (#000000) pixel consumes zero power.** Dark apps with true black backgrounds can significantly reduce screen power consumption compared to the same app with a white or colored background.

---

## LCD Dark Mode vs. OLED Dark Mode: A Critical Distinction

Here's what confuses many users: **dark mode on an LCD doesn't save battery**. When a phone with an LCD panel switches to dark mode — showing a dark grey or near-black background — the backlight is still illuminated at full brightness behind that dark screen. The LCD panel blocks more light, but the power source (the backlight) continues consuming energy.

**Only OLED phones genuinely benefit from dark themes**, and only when those themes use **true black** (#000000), not dark grey (#1A1A1A or similar).

| Display Type | Dark Theme Battery Savings | True Black Required |
|---|---|---|
| LCD / IPS | None | N/A |
| OLED (Google Pixel, Samsung Galaxy S, OnePlus, etc.) | Yes — significant | Yes |
| AMOLED (Samsung's OLED) | Yes — identical to OLED | Yes |
| Mini-LED | Minimal (zone dimming) | No |

